Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ Here is a C++ function that accomplishes the task:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string convert_markdown_to_html(std::string markdown_text) {
    std::string html_text;
    std::stringstream ss(markdown_text);
    std::string line;

    while (std::getline(ss, line)) {
        if (line.find("<") != std::string::npos) {
            html_text += line;
        } else {
            std::stringstream ss2(line);
            std::string tag;
            while (std::getline(ss2, tag, '>')) {
                html_text += tag;
            }
        }
    }

    return html_text;
}
```

This function takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text as input. It then iterates through each line of the markdown text and checks if it contains a `<` tag. If it does, the line is added to the `html_text` variable as is. If it doesn't, the function creates a new std::stringstream from the line and reads each tag until it encounters a `>` character. The tags are then added to the `html_text` variable. Finally, the function returns the `html_text` containing the converted HTML.

